Split manufacturing for starship separates booster and upper stage lines to cut serial bottlenecks in final assembly. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 92. The approach divides fabrication of the super heavy booster from work on the ship upper stage into distinct facilities. Tooling and space allocations are moving early to enable this separation. The goal is to let each stage move forward independently rather than competing for the same assembly hall at starbase.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:50.220
This setup aims to ease the constraints that have held back the rated of test flights. At Kennedy Space Center, a transporter system now carries starship sections between processing buildings and the LC-39A launch pad. It enables horizontal integration and stacking for hardware that used to require starbase facilities.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:01:01.360
Tests show it manages both booster and upper stage pieces without added heavy cranes. The addition gives Florida a second location for ground operations on starship.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:11.460
Horizontal transport cuts the need for vertical lifting gear at the site. The starbase police department is switching its fleet to all electric vehicles for site duties.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:20.340
Charging stations are being installed to keep operations running around the clock. Power comes from generation already on site for production work.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:36.100
Range and capacity checks match the roads and perimeter needs. Starshield hardware is entering department of defense command networks as the main data pathway. It provides low latency connections beyond what older military satellites can supply in volume.

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:46.580
Current contracts include relay duties and sensor data returns from several orbit planes. The hardware is set to handle multiple classified missions as the standard link.

